<body>
  <h1>Mental Health Sentiment Analyzer - Final Documentation Report</h1>

  <h2>1. Project Objective</h2>
  <p>
    The objective of this project is to build a Natural Language Processing system that analyzes employee mental health survey responses,
    classifies sentiment, identifies at-risk employee cohorts, and presents insights through a professional Streamlit dashboard.
  </p>
  <p>
    The project supports HR teams by summarizing workplace wellness patterns from employee survey data and by providing a simple interface
    for analyzing new employee feedback.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Manager Requirement Coverage</h2>
  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Requirement</th>
      <th>Status</th>
      <th>Project File / Output</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Build the model</td>
      <td>Completed</td>
      <td><code>models/sentiment_tfidf_pipeline.pkl</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Develop Streamlit application with professional UI</td>
      <td>Completed</td>
      <td><code>app.py</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Model should provide proper outputs</td>
      <td>Completed with documented limitations</td>
      <td>TF-IDF model + polarity/risk-term safeguard in Streamlit app</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Prepare documentation report</td>
      <td>Completed</td>
      <td><code>reports/final_documentation_report.doc</code> and <code>reports/final_documentation_report.md</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Include methodology, results, accuracy, achievements</td>
      <td>Completed</td>
      <td>This report</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Prepare files for ZIP submission</td>
      <td>Completed</td>
      <td>Data, models, app, reports, visuals, notebooks, scripts</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h2>3. Dataset</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Dataset:</strong> OSMI Mental Health in Tech Survey</li>
    <li><strong>Source:</strong> Kaggle</li>
    <li><strong>Raw records:</strong> 1,259</li>
    <li><strong>Cleaned records:</strong> 1,250</li>
    <li><strong>Usable free-text comment records:</strong> 160</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The dataset contains employee demographics, workplace mental health support indicators, treatment status, work interference,
    and optional free-text comments.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Work Completed</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Loaded and inspected the raw survey dataset.</li>
    <li>Checked missing values, duplicate records, and inconsistent values.</li>
    <li>Cleaned invalid age values and standardized gender categories.</li>
    <li>Created a cleaned survey dataset.</li>
    <li>Performed exploratory data analysis.</li>
    <li>Generated visualizations for age, gender, country, family history, treatment, work interference, sentiment distribution, model comparison, and confusion matrix.</li>
    <li>Preprocessed free-text employee comments.</li>
    <li>Generated sentiment labels using TextBlob polarity because the dataset does not contain manual sentiment labels.</li>
    <li>Converted text comments into TF-IDF numerical features.</li>
    <li>Trained Naive Bayes, Logistic Regression, Linear SVM, and tuned Logistic Regression models.</li>
    <li>Compared models using Accuracy and Weighted F1 score.</li>
    <li>Selected the best sentiment model.</li>
    <li>Exported the trained NLP pipeline.</li>
    <li>Created sentiment analysis, at-risk cohort, and HR recommendation reports.</li>
    <li>Developed a professional Streamlit dashboard application.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Methodology</h2>
  <h3>5.1 Data Cleaning</h3>
  <p>
    The raw dataset was cleaned by removing invalid age values, standardizing gender values, filling important missing categorical fields,
    and keeping usable employee comments for NLP analysis.
  </p>

  <h3>5.2 Text Preprocessing</h3>
  <p>
    Employee comments were converted to lowercase, URLs were removed, special characters and numbers were removed, and extra spaces were normalized.
  </p>

  <h3>5.3 Sentiment Label Creation</h3>
  <p>
    The dataset does not provide sentiment labels. Therefore, TextBlob polarity was used to create labels:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Positive:</strong> polarity greater than 0.05</li>
    <li><strong>Negative:</strong> polarity less than -0.05</li>
    <li><strong>Neutral:</strong> polarity between -0.05 and 0.05</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>5.4 Feature Extraction</h3>
  <p>
    TF-IDF Vectorization was used to convert cleaned text comments into numerical features for model training.
  </p>

  <h3>5.5 Model Training and Selection</h3>
  <p>
    Multiple classification models were trained and compared. The final model was selected using Weighted F1 score because it gives
    a better view of performance across all sentiment classes than accuracy alone.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Model Results</h2>
  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Model</th>
      <th>Accuracy</th>
      <th>Weighted F1</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Logistic Regression</td>
      <td>0.425</td>
      <td>0.4197</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Tuned Logistic Regression</td>
      <td>0.425</td>
      <td>0.4072</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Naive Bayes</td>
      <td>0.500</td>
      <td>0.3745</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Linear SVM</td>
      <td>0.375</td>
      <td>0.3619</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h2>7. Selected Model</h2>
  <p>
    The selected model is <strong>TF-IDF Vectorizer + Logistic Regression</strong>.
    It was selected because it achieved the highest Weighted F1 score among the tested sentiment models.
  </p>
  <p>
    Exported model file: <code>models/sentiment_tfidf_pipeline.pkl</code>
  </p>

  <h2>8. Streamlit Application</h2>
  <p>
    The Streamlit application is implemented in <code>app.py</code>. It includes the following pages:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Executive Overview</li>
    <li>Sentiment Analysis</li>
    <li>At-Risk Cohorts</li>
    <li>Model Performance</li>
    <li>Live Sentiment Prediction</li>
    <li>Reports Viewer</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For new comment prediction, the app displays the trained model output and applies a polarity/risk-term safeguard so short high-risk phrases
    such as stress, burnout, anxiety, depression, and trust issues are flagged more appropriately.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Key Findings</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The cleaned dataset contains 1,250 survey responses.</li>
    <li>Only 160 rows contain usable comments, which limits the sentiment model accuracy.</li>
    <li>Positive comments are the largest sentiment group, but negative comments are still meaningful for wellness review.</li>
    <li>Employees with family history show higher treatment-seeking behavior.</li>
    <li>Employees reporting work interference are more likely to show mental health concerns.</li>
    <li>Role-based analysis could not be completed because the dataset does not contain a role/job-title column.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>10. Achievements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Completed an end-to-end NLP workflow.</li>
    <li>Built and exported a reusable sentiment classification pipeline.</li>
    <li>Created a professional Streamlit dashboard for analysis and prediction.</li>
    <li>Prepared final documentation and supporting reports.</li>
    <li>Organized datasets, models, reports, notebooks, scripts, and visuals for final ZIP submission.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>11. Limitations</h2>
  <div class="warning">
    The model is a project-level NLP workflow and should not be treated as a medical, clinical, or employment decision system.
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li>Sentiment labels are generated using TextBlob, not manually annotated by domain experts.</li>
    <li>The free-text comment dataset is small, which affects model accuracy.</li>
    <li>The app is intended to support HR review, not automate final decisions.</li>
    <li>Role-based cohort analysis is unavailable due to missing role data.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>12. Final Submission Contents</h2>
  <p>The final ZIP submission should contain:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>app.py</code></li>
    <li><code>requirements.txt</code></li>
    <li><code>README.md</code></li>
    <li><code>Data/</code></li>
    <li><code>models/</code></li>
    <li><code>reports/</code></li>
    <li><code>visuals/</code></li>
    <li><code>Notebooks/</code></li>
    <li><code>scripts/</code></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>13. How to Run</h2>
  <p>Install dependencies:</p>
  <p><code>pip install -r requirements.txt</code></p>
  <p>Run the Streamlit app:</p>
  <p><code>python -m streamlit run app.py</code></p>

  <h2>14. Final Conclusion</h2>
  <div class="note">
    The project satisfies the manager's requirements: model building, professional Streamlit application, documentation report,
    model files, datasets, and supporting files are prepared for final ZIP submission.
  </div>
</body>
